---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import LessonNav from '../../../components/LessonNav.astro';
import LessonEyebrow from '../../../components/LessonEyebrow.astro';
import Diagram from '../../../components/Diagram.astro';
import RuleBand from '../../../components/RuleBand.astro';
import { ladder } from '../../../data/ladder.js';
import { finderPromise } from '../../../data/quiz.js';

// GAINED 2026-07-29: the "how much money at which temperature" material from the
// retired hot-vs-cold page. Deciding what lives hot and what lives cold is a SETUP
// decision, so it belongs on the ladder; what hot and cold MEAN is a property of a
// wallet and stayed in /learn/bitcoin-keys §6.
//
// MERGED 2026-07-30 — the four rung pages fold in here, and this is now the whole
// lesson. The rung pages diverted the reader from the linear learning
// process," they carried Take-action affordances inside Learn (a save-to-plan band,
// "Choose your device →"), and they sat OUTSIDE curriculum.js — no position, no
// LessonNav, no next. Clicking one dropped you off the course.
//
// The merge only became viable once the fat came out, and it was mostly one thing:
// every rung page carried an `inheritance` block, while /learn/inheritance §2 covers
// the SAME FOUR SETUPS with the same argument, seven lessons later. That is cut
// here and named in plain text. Single-sig's "how to set it up" walkthrough is cut
// too — Learn teaches, /checklist acts — after its one uncovered step (set a device
// PIN) was added there, so nothing left the site.
//
// The comparison table was already sitting in ladder.js: `solves` and `introduces`
// were written as one-liners per rung and had never been rendered side by side.
//
// Shamir and BIP-85 moved OUT to /deep-dive/*, where the 201 tier already holds
// their demonstrations and already calls itself "optional, skippable."
//
// Two rules live on this page (03 simplest-setup, 04 savings-offline) — RuleBand
// renders both; don't assume one.
const rungs = ladder;
---
<Base
  title="The wallet configuration ladder · BitcoinKeys.guide"
  description="Every serious Bitcoin self-custody setup fits on one ladder of rising complexity — from single-signature to collaborative multisig. What each rung solves, what it costs you, and how to find the lowest one that covers you."
  verified="2026-07-31"
>
  <section class="prose">
    <LessonEyebrow />
    <h1>The wallet configuration <span class="accent">ladder</span></h1>
    <p class="lead">The major self-custody setups arrange themselves into a ladder of rising complexity. Each rung solves a real problem — and introduces new ways to fail.</p>
    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">How to use it</span>
      <p>Don’t ask “what’s the most secure setup?” Ask “<strong>what’s the simplest setup that adequately covers the risks I can actually name?</strong>” Find that rung, build it, test it — and don’t climb higher until a real reason pushes you up. Complexity you don’t fully control is itself a threat.</p>
      <p style="margin-bottom:0">But this isn’t a decision you make once and never revisit. <strong>The clearest reason to climb is that the amount you’re securing has outgrown the rung you’re on</strong> — if your holdings have multiplied since you set this up, and thinking about them sitting on a single key now makes you uneasy, that unease <em>is</em> the real reason. It counts. Move up deliberately, one rung, and test the new setup as carefully as you tested the first.</p>
    </div>
    <RuleBand />
    <p class="sub">New to keys, seed words, and what “signing” means? <a href="/learn/bitcoin-keys">Go back to what a key actually is →</a></p>
  </section>



  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The four rungs at a glance</h2>
    <p class="sub">Read down the last two columns. Every rung buys you the third and charges you the fourth — that trade is the whole ladder.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <p class="scroll-hint">Swipe sideways to see every column →</p>
    <div class="scroll tbl-wrap">
      <table class="rungtbl">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Rung</th><th>Best for</th><th>Rough cost</th><th>What it solves</th><th>What it costs you</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {rungs.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">
                <a href={`#rung-${r.step}`}><span class="rt-n mono">{r.step}</span> {r.short}</a>
              </th>
              <td>{r.forWho}</td>
              <td class="mono rt-cost">{r.cost}</td>
              <td set:html={r.solves}></td>
              <td set:html={r.introduces}></td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  {rungs.map((r) => (
    <section class="prose rung-sec" id={`rung-${r.step}`}>
      <p class="rung-eyebrow mono">Rung {r.step} of {rungs.length}</p>
      <h2>{r.pageName ?? r.name}</h2>
      <p class="lead rung-lead">{r.tagline}</p>

      {r.diagram && <Diagram name={r.diagram} />}

      <p set:html={r.whatItIs}></p>

      <h3>What it gains you</h3>
      <ul>{(r.gains ?? []).map((g) => <li set:html={g}></li>)}</ul>

      <h3>What it costs you</h3>
      <ul>{(r.costs ?? []).map((c) => <li set:html={c}></li>)}</ul>

      {r.keyRisk && (
        <div class="callout warn">
          <span class="label">{r.keyRisk.label}</span>
          <p set:html={r.keyRisk.body}></p>
        </div>
      )}

      {r.keyNote && (
        <div class:list={['callout', r.keyNote.tone ?? '']}>
          <span class="label">{r.keyNote.label}</span>
          <p set:html={r.keyNote.body}></p>
        </div>
      )}

      {r.also && (
        <>
          <h3>{r.also.label}</h3>
          <p set:html={r.also.body}></p>
        </>
      )}

      <h3>Who should use it</h3>
      <p set:html={r.whoShould}></p>

      <h3>When to climb</h3>
      <p set:html={r.whenToClimb}></p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Reading the ladder</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Most newcomers</strong> belong on rung 1 (single-signature) and should stay there through their first year.</li>
      <li><strong>Most substantial holders</strong> settle at rung 3 or 4 (self-run or collaborative multi-signature).</li>
      <li><strong>Bigger multi-signature (3-of-5)</strong> is right for a small minority — large holdings or genuine multi-party needs. It’s the same technology as 2-of-3, so it lives on the multi-signature rung above rather than as a separate step.</li>
      <li>Jumping straight to the top introduces complexity you’re not yet equipped to manage. That complexity is a bigger risk than the ones it’s meant to solve.</li>
    </ul>


    <div class="callout">
      <span class="label">Which rung is yours?</span>
      <p>This page teaches the ladder; it doesn’t place you on it. The <a href="/find-your-setup">setup finder</a> does that — {finderPromise}, and it names the rung that fits what you’re actually protecting against. It errs toward the <em>lowest</em> rung that covers you, because that’s the rule this whole ladder is built on.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="cta-row">
      <a href="/find-your-setup" class="btn btn-primary">Find my rung <span class="arw">→</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <LessonNav />
</Base>

<style>
  section { margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .cta-row { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.7rem; margin-top: 1.4rem; }

  /* The at-a-glance table. The shared .scroll wrapper (global.css) gives it the
     sideways scroll, edge fade, and sticky header/first-column on phones. */
  .tbl-wrap { margin: 0 0 0.5rem; }
  .rungtbl { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.9rem; min-width: 44rem; }
  .rungtbl th, .rungtbl td { text-align: left; vertical-align: top; padding: 0.7rem 0.8rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); }
  .rungtbl thead th { font-family: var(--mono); font-size: 0.72rem; letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--accent); border-bottom-color: var(--accent); }
  .rungtbl tbody th { font-weight: 600; white-space: nowrap; }
  .rungtbl tbody th a { text-decoration: none; color: var(--text); }
  .rungtbl tbody th a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
  .rt-n { color: var(--accent); font-weight: 800; margin-right: 0.3rem; }
  .rt-cost { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.82rem; white-space: nowrap; }
  /* On a phone the sticky first column already takes a third of the screen —
     a nowrap cost string ("$59–$249 (same device)") must not claim another. */
  @media (max-width: 760px) { .rt-cost { white-space: normal; } }
  .rungtbl tbody tr:last-child th, .rungtbl tbody tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }

  /* Each rung is a scannable block with its own anchor — the action side deep-links
     to these (checklist steps, quiz "how →" links), so the ids are load-bearing. */
  .rung-sec { scroll-margin-top: 4.5rem; border-top: 2px solid var(--line); padding-top: 1.6rem; margin-top: 2.2rem; }
  .rung-eyebrow { color: var(--accent); font-size: 0.78rem; letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase; margin: 0 0 0.2rem; }
  .rung-lead { font-size: 1.02rem; margin-top: 0.2rem; }
  .rung-sec h3 { font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 1.3rem 0 0.4rem; }
</style>
